re  so for each concentration that's  reported in the software you'll get a  confidence interval as well and so then  you can directly see  in your measurements if I'm getting an  uncertainty that's simply larger than I  would be comfortable with you'll know  that you can then just dilute that  sample run it again because as you get  to lower concentrations your relative  Precision is going to be better  and that's also reminds me of something  I commonly tell customers if they aren't  quite sure about where the concentration  is going to be of the sample they're  dealing with and they're concerned with  this issue instead of running technical  replicates on digital PCR I advise to  run delusional replicates take that  sample and run it as is and then also do  a 10 or 100 full dilution of it as well  and run that as on the same experiment  or even a different experiment and then  you'll get more information and if one  of those is outside of the dynamic range  the other one could be and if they're  both inside the dynamic range you should  see great agreement between them and get  even more confidence in your measurement  fantastic thank you your next question  how does the instrument perform the  mentioned Auto false positive rejection  yeah the auto false positive rejection  is important for any experiment but it's  really critical when we're talking about  rare targets because again with rare  targets if you're only expecting or  commonly seeing 10 of those marker  Chambers being lit up you really want to  know that all 10 of those are true  positives because two of them being  false positives can have a huge impact  on the relative difference in our answer  so the absolute Q system has actually  two ways to help with that  with auto false positives uh one is that  the instrument actually takes two images  so there's the end point detection image  that we've been talking about but  there's actually another image  collection step really early on in PCR  cycling to get the background  fluorescence levels from that particular  sample on that particular plate and this  is collected for every single individual  experiment and then subtracted out  from the end point  uh image so that means if there was any  fluorescent artifact present it would  get subtracted away meaning it would not  contribute to a false positive because  it would get subtracted out and there's  also another more sophisticated layer  where the software isn't just looking at  the overall intensity of fluorescence  it's also looking at the inside that  image the pixel by pixel  intensity of fluorescent signal that  distribution of fluorescence intensity  within the marker chamber and it knows  that there's certain patterns that  represent real PCR amplification versus  perhaps an artifact so that's also used  to make sure that things called as  positive  truly reflect PCR fluorescence not  artifacts  all right thank you Dave we've got time  for a couple more questions  your next question do I need to redesign  my existing real-time PCR assays or  change how I typically design assays to  use absolute Q  I generally know the oh the underlying  biochemistry is really the same from  digital and real-time PCR  so if you have PCR assays that you or  real-time PSIs that you've been using  those generally can be used with digital  PCR with no optimization no change  no change I have walked into plenty of  customer labs and we just wanted to try  the real-time asset that they've been  working with we run it on the absolute  queue and the data is perfect no need to  optimize and again that's because the  underlying biochemistry is pretty much  the same  and in terms of developing any newer  assays really the guidelines that you  would use for designing an assay with  real time would also apply to digital so  anything about the primer sequences the  length the sequence context all that is  pretty much going to be the same for  digital as it was for real time  all right great thank you and your last  question today what is dead volume and  why is it important for a digital PCR  yeah we briefly alluded to the idea of  dead volume where let's say in a system  we're going to load 10 microliters of  our reaction mix into the instrument or  into the plate to get experiment get  assessed  some instruments might take say 10  microliters and then only actually  utilize and get data from a fraction of  that maybe half that volume or something  like that because some of that volume  maybe wasn't effectively incorporated  into a sub-reaction and if that  sub-reaction isn't usable for one reason  or another and it doesn't make it to the  final end of the data analysis that  volume from that sub-reaction is  considered dead volume or any other  volume that never makes it into any  sub-reaction at all dead volume  with the absolute Q system we talked  about that there's  over 95 percent of the volume is truly  utilized and actually gets data  associated with it so that's less than  five percent of that being dead volume  and that's important for two reasons one  is reagents they cost money that dead  volume consists of your assay mix and  your master mix so that's money going  away secondly if you're looking at rare  targets if you've only assessing 50 of  what you loaded you're going to have a  lot more uncertainty a lot more  variability and if I detected my target  was it in that 10 microliters but not in  the five Market leaders assessed now you  just have another level of variability  another sub sampling issue to keeping  track of whereas with our system we're  having over 99 percent  actually 95 of that volume utilized so  we don't have that concern  well thank you again Dave for your time  today and your wonderful presentation we  would also like to thank lab roots and  our sponsor of Thermo Fisher Scientific  for underwriting today's educational  webcast  before we go I'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and  further interesting questions  we did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed by the speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand  labords will alert you via email when  it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event until next time  goodbye

